  "dom_id": "step-q109014",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "The substitution $z={y}^{m}$ for some integer $m$ transforms the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+3xy=-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{\u2032}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $P(x)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=3x,\\,q(x)=-{x}^{4},\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}+3x{y}^{-2}=-{x}^{4}.\\,\\,(\u2217)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(\u2217)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3xz=-{x}^{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the above equation in standard form, which gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-6xz=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=-6x.$",
